# migrated from tests/cases/abduce.chat (gen346, hermetic + isolated)
[mock live]
# gen505y — R1 (TEST_TODO §0.0): la KB intera, non un contesto amputato.
!set PARROT0_BASE=kb/core/base.p0
!set PARROT0_SESSION=
!set PARROT0_WORLD_FACTS=1
!set PARROT0_LANG=en
!set PARROT0_PROFILE=kb/profiles/agi.p0
!reset

[test abduce]
# gen131 (L16/L19): ABDUCTION — inference to the missing premise, the inverse of
# deduction and the mirror of gen129's subtraction. gen129 removed a belief and
# watched a conclusion fall; abduction starts from a conclusion that does NOT
# hold and runs the rule engine BACKWARDS to name the belief that would entail
# it. Computed from the real rules (kb_rule_body_preds), not guessed.
#
# gen505y — R2 (TEST_TODO §0.0): con la KB intera socrate E' un uomo ed e'
# mortale, quindi il campione «socrates / man / mortal» era invecchiato: la
# catena si prova su nomi che nessuno puo' conoscere. L'intento — l'abduzione
# nomina la premessa mancante, e la premessa fornita chiude il cerchio — e'
# lo stesso.

# No rule concludes "zorbulator", so there is honestly nothing that would make it so.
> what would make zibbo a zorbulator?
< I have no rule that would make anything a zorbulator, so I can't say what would.

# Teach the rule but NOT its premise: zibbo is not (yet) provably a glimmer.
> every florp is a glimmer
< Learned rule: glimmer(X) :- florp(X).
> is zibbo a glimmer?
<! Yes

# Abduction: the goal fails, so name the premise that WOULD entail it.
> what would make zibbo a glimmer?
< If you told me that zibbo is a florp, then zibbo would be a glimmer — that's the rule florp -> glimmer.

# Supply exactly that premise — and gen103's self-correction volunteers the
# downstream consequence the abduction predicted. Two generations composing.
> zibbo is a florp
< Learned: zibbo is a florp. Now zibbo is a glimmer after all.
> is zibbo a glimmer?
< Yes.
# gen135: contrastive why-not uses the same missing-premise analysis, but now
# explains a failed conclusion directly instead of requiring "what would make".
> every friendly dog is a goodboy
< Learned rule: goodboy(X) :- friendly(X), dog(X).
> rex is a dog
< Learned: rex is a dog.
> why isn't rex a goodboy?
< rex is not a goodboy because I am missing: rex is a friendly. The rule is friendly and dog -> goodboy.


# Now the goal HOLDS, so the same question is deduction, not abduction: it cites
# the real proof instead of an antecedent.
> why might zibbo be a glimmer?
< I already conclude that: glimmer(zibbo) because florp(zibbo).
!forget florp(zibbo)
!forget dog(rex)
